    "gap_findings": {
      "step_3": "No malicious process can be confirmed from the available incident data. The raw NetWitness digest explicitly says process fields were absent across all 67 alerts, and the evidence is purely network-level Modbus traffic (192.168.0.19 to 192.168.0.34 on port 502, actions 'preset single register' / 'data push'). That means there is no direct evidence of a spawned process, parent-child execution chain, or endpoint artifact indicating malware execution on the victim host.",
      "step_4": "A process tree analysis cannot be completed from the available evidence because there are no process, user, or host telemetry fields in the alert set. There is also no sign of privilege escalation, lateral movement, or data exfiltration in the endpoint sense; the only observable behavior is repeated internal Modbus communication between two RFC1918 hosts on the same network range, which is more consistent with industrial-control or remote-device interaction than executable process activity. However, because the traffic is between internal hosts and repeated at high volume, lateral movement remains a weak possibility only in the networking sense, not confirmed by process telemetry.",
      "step_1": "Identity details are largely missing. Username is not present anywhere in the alert telemetry, and login details are not available. The source IP is 192.168.0.19 and destination IP is 192.168.0.34; another source IP mentioned in the case metadata is 192.168.20.14, but TI explicitly did not evaluate it and it is not linked to the alerts. Computer name and operating system are also absent. Based on the alert title 'Modbus Non-Whitelisted Source' and the destination port 502, the environment likely involves an OT/ICS device or controller, but that is only contextual inference and does not resolve hostname or OS.",
      "mitre_redirection": "The evidence does not support the initial focus on endpoint malware execution or confirmed lateral movement. The strongest supported classification is network-based suspicious industrial protocol activity: Modbus traffic with a non-whitelisted source. The corrected tactic is best aligned to Command and Control or Lateral Movement only at a weak/inferential level; however, because there is no process or authentication evidence, the most defensible classification is suspicious internal network activity involving industrial control communications, not verified malware."
